Despite acting since she was three, bratpacker Molly Ringwald was daunted when asked to tell a deeply personal story live on stage. She explains how hip New York storytelling group The Moth persuaded her, and why you should see them in London
I had been an admirer of The Moth for years before I became one of its storytellers. In the summer of 2003, a friend invited me at the last minute to attend a Moth show at The Players, a 19th-century gentlemen's social club in Lower Manhattan. It was standing room only, but I was mercifully given a seat because I was pregnant. Hugely pregnant. The kind of pregnant that inspired cab drivers to call out at me on the street. "Hang in there, lady! Any day now!" I was not expecting to be interested and yet I found myself engaged. When do you go out, and something you've never heard of is so good? Never.
Also, I was sitting down. When you're pregnant, that counts for a lot.
Although I spent the night enthralled by the moving, unpredictable and fearlessly candid tales, afterwards I declined an invitation to tell one myself. I had been performing since I was three years old, but the thought of standing alone on a stage without lyrics or lines to protect me seemed wildly unappealing. Besides, I didn't have a story 15 minutes long; after years lived in public, I'd had so much practice speaking in soundbites (to avoid having my words taken out of context) that I didn't think it was even possible to talk for that long.
Then, nearly nine years later, I stood on a stage in North Carolina and told my own story, about a painfully difficult year that I'd experienced with my child; the same one I was pregnant with back in 2003. When my daughter was seven years old, she became practically unrecognisable to me. She was angry and volatile and yet seemingly unfazed by the social isolation her behaviour created. My story was about how I finally realised what I could do to help her.
The Moth was founded in 1997 by the poet and writer George Dawes Green, who was raised in Georgia in the deep south – where being a great raconteur is practically a birthright. His idea was to bring to the stage that feeling of sitting on the porch on warm summer evenings with good friends, long after dinner has ended, and sharing stories late into the night while the… that's right, moths flit around the lights. Over the years, The Moth has grown and evolved, but it has never wavered from its original aim.
"Live, on stage, without notes" is the main directive. Each story is roughly 15 minutes long (a local musician sits with the storyteller and gives gentle musical prompts should they exceed their allotted time). If this sounds like a nerve-racking exercise, it is. It is also like a boot camp for story structure, which is perhaps why it has attracted some of the most respected writers of the 21st century, including Salman Rushdie, Nathan Englander, Neil Gaiman, Annie Proulx and the late George Plimpton. Yet variety of life experience is as important as eloquence, and fire fighters, astrophysicists, cartoonists, carpenters, musicians, chefs and former pickpockets have all appeared on its simple stage.
Molly Ringwald tells her story in 2013: ��When my daughter was seven she became unrecognisable to me.�� Photograph: Alexis Wiener
Being a writer doesn't necessarily give you an advantage. Writing is a very solitary act, away from the collaborative call-and-response energy of the audience. When writers work, their words are laboured over; hours, even days can be spent deliberating over a comma. When telling a story live, no such luxury exists. One could memorise lines, sure, but this practice is discouraged, and for good reason. It becomes something else; it becomes theatre, and it's the unscripted spontaneity that attracts. One veteran storyteller, writer Elna Baker, puts it like this: "A night at The Moth gives you that same nervous feeling that you get at a really great dinner party. Like anything can go wrong."
And sometimes it does. The essayist Adam Gopnik, who has told multiple Moth stories, describes the challenges of working on his first. "I took it much too casually and told a very mediocre story. Later I went back and took seriously what they said… searching for the inner life of the story, the microdrama, trying to make sure… to use all of those cliched terms, that the stakes were high enough, the characters clearly drawn and so on. Instead of being impatient, I listened and learned a great deal about how to make a story work."
While some storytellers may underestimate the process (as Gopnik explains, "I noticed that this is a recurring pattern, in a sense, of learning anything: you have to find a little bit of humility, recognise that you don't know how to do this thing before you do it") many others find themselves apprehensive and even overwhelmed. In fact, almost every storyteller I spoke with (from among my personal favourites) was initially reticent.
Comedian/actor Mike Birbiglia's career was kickstarted by the stories he told. His very first story, in 2003, about an especially awkward love triangle, became his one-man show, My Girlfriend's Boyfriend, which enjoyed a successful run at the Soho Theatre in London; a later story, about a bizarre sleep disorder that at one point caused him to jump out of a motel window, was recently turned into a film, Sleepwalk With Me, in which he starred and directed. "There was a video made of that [first] story," he recalls, "and it's really painful to watch. I was actually quivering. It's funny, because it was so hard for me to say, but now it just rolls off the tongue."
It's precisely that vulnerability that draws people in. Unlike the glut of social media, where people can pretend to be anything they like, or reality television, which most people understand is entirely manufactured, it's the revelation and unflinching nakedness within the best Moth story that you can't turn away from.
"Human beings are hard-wired for stories," says The Moth's artistic director, Catherine Burns. "There is something about the quality of the human voice that really connects us. Coming out to a show, and looking at that person's face as they tell that story. In many cases," she says, "it's by their neighbour. It's one of the reasons why we always have locals [local theatres], because we want people to hear stories from people in their community."
'Live, on stage, without notes is the main directive. If this sounds like a nerve-racking exercise, it is.' Photograph: Caroll Taveras for the Guardian
Years ago I played a mix I made for my jazz musician father. At the end of a long recording of Benny Goodman at Carnegie Hall, I had edited out the applause to get to the next song, and my father was horrified. "The applause is part of the performance," he insisted.
Catherine agrees. "When we started the radio show, a lot of people wanted us to bring people [together] in a room and read a written version of their story. It seems so obvious now that a huge part of what we are doing is defined by having that live audience. It's so much more dynamic."
Of course, for the one standing in front of that audience, it can be anxiety-inducing. Even astronaut Michael Massimino admits to being nervous, and this from a guy who serviced the Hubble telescope in space with the entire world watching. Conservationist Alan Rabinowitz told the story of how he overcame severe stuttering as a child to go on to create the world's first jaguar reserve. Recalling that night, he describes the "incredible feeling of sharing in the most personal manner possible. I felt as if for the first time in my life, I could speak the story to people who cared and empathised. It was a kind of healing that I never quite felt writing the stories on paper."
All this can sound a bit like therapy, and of course other people's therapy is not very interesting – like looking at pictures of someone else's kids, or listening to a person recount the dream they had the night before. I consider myself a naturally curious person, but I confess to having written off a potential suitor years ago after he recounted a long, rambling dream on my answering machine. (Or maybe it was the fact that my machine cut him off, and he called back to finish it?)
