The Sox clinched their third consecutive series win with a 10-hit attack against All-Star Trevor Cahill, whom Guillen called a Cy Young Award candidate.
They were only 1-for-6 with runners in scoring position, and Juan Pierre was picked off first base and grounded into two double plays. But the Sox are gradually finding ways to overcome the mishaps that cost them dearly in the four weeks prior to their successful trip.
"It's a start," said Sergio Santos, who retired Josh Willingham on a force play with the tying and winning runs on base to notch his fifth save in as many chances. "We knew it wasn't going to happen overnight. We knew we wouldn't win 24 games in a row. It starts with series. We're looking to win series, and the rest will take care of itself."
The Sox also are capitalizing on opponents' mistakes. Cahill committed a throwing error on a sacrifice bunt, leading to two runs in the seventh that made the difference in the outcome. It compensated for the missed scoring chances against Cahill in four of the first five innings.
