The afterlife of an "American Idol" contestant is often brutish and short. For every also-ran carving out a modest career as a country singer (Josh Gracin, season two, fourth place) or Lane Bryant spokesmodel (Kimberley Locke, season two, third place), there are three working at county fairs--if they're lucky.
A year from now, Constantine Maroulis (season four, sixth place) really, really wants you to have still heard of him. He knows that he faces a Job-like struggle against the forces of pop-cultural indifference.
But he has a plan.
Maroulis doesn't want to be like Clay Aiken, who has recently turned vague and imperious. And he certainly doesn't want to be like Ryan Starr, who played a corpse on an episode of "C.S.I." and hasn't been heard from since--as apt a metaphor for the perilous second acts of ex-"Idol" hopefuls as you're likely to find.
What Maroulis really wants is to be like Roseanne. Besides working on an upcoming solo album, a possible book and ongoing tour with his rock band, Pray for the Soul of Betty, Maroulis is starring in an ABC sitcom pilot. The show, from Kelsey Grammer's production company, is inspired by Maroulis' life as the black-sheep son of his extended Greek family.
The show's creators "found me to be an interesting persona," he explains.
Maroulis, 30, is older, cheerier and savvier than the average "Idol" contestant, with awareness of his appeal that verges on encyclopedic. This is how he explains his place in the season four: "I wasn't too dangerous a [rocker] to be outside [audiences'] realm of understanding but I wasn't too much from the heartland, either. I was just me.... I've worked my ass off but I'm educated, come from a good family and I'm cultured. You can talk to me; I'm respectful and humble."
Maroulis grew up in Brooklyn, a long-haired semi-outcast in his working class family. "We've overcome a lot of tragedy, and `American Idol' sort of brought us together," he recalls. "I was always the black sheep of the family, you know, with my long hair. I come from a pretty strict Greek Orthodox family and they weren't into that. I was always struggling to get their attention. They sat me at the kids' table until I was well into my twenties. I get to sit at the grownups' table now, though."
After attending the Boston Conservatory, Maroulis did stints on Broadway, off-Broadway and off-off-Broadway, playing everyone from Roger in "Rent" to Tom Joad in "The Grapes of Wrath."
Maroulis joined Betty before auditioning for "Idol." His bandmates were "a little bit" nervous that their new lead singer would disappear forever into the show's gaping maw, "but I think they embraced it eventually," he says. It helped that Betty appeared briefly on the show. "They were like, `Isn't it crazy when people recognize you on the street?' And I'm like, `Yeah, you're telling me. You were on the show for 15 seconds. I was on for 15 weeks,'" Maroulis says.
Maroulis flunked out of "Idol" after a memorable rendition of Nickelback's "How You Remind Me." Conspiracy theories abounded; Paula Abdul cried. Bo Bice, Maroulis' roommate during the competition, famously suggested that losing might be a better career move than winning. "I don't believe him," Maroulis says now. "Bo's one of my best friends. But all of us wanted to win."
Soon after Maroulis left "Idol," Betty officially issued their self-titled debut, recorded before the season began. Given Maroulis' budding solo stardom, it's entirely possible that this will be their only album ever, though Maroulis says he hopes not.
"I've always been some part of an ensemble whether it's the band or the show or whatever. Because of the nature of the show I was thrust into this solo status, and I dig it. I've come into my own musically and emotionally. I think this is the time for me to do my solo stuff. Earlier, in my twenties, I couldn't have done that. I couldn't have held the room." (That sound you hear is the members of Pray For Betty weeping to themselves).
There's also a solo record in the offing. "I can't talk too much about that," says Maroulis, who promises "an eclectic mix" of rock, pop and R&B. If all goes well, the disc's release will dovetail with the possible summer debut of Maroulis' sitcom.
