Plans to build a sunken motorway down the middle of Parramatta Road have been shelved after construction companies told the government it would be more expensive than digging a tunnel.
The advice means the O'Farrell government's signature WestConnex motorway will have to be built largely underground, requiring more controversial exhaust stacks in the inner west.
And it potentially increases the cost of WestConnex by billions, even before a detailed plan for the project has been drawn up.
Multiple sources have said last year's vision of a ''slot'' motorway running through Parramatta Road from Taverners Hill to where the existing M4 starts at Strathfield would not work.
