Share on Twitter TWEET
Link Controversial: Building the WestConnex motorway could result in a proliferation of unsightly pollution stacks. Photo: Steven Siewert
It would also have to remove utilities such as water and sewerage underneath Parramatta Road, and spend years managing the traffic during construction. The extra costs would outweigh any price benefit from cutting a motorway in the ground rather than drilling a tunnel.
If built entirely as a tunnel, however, the WestConnex motorway would still ''surface'' at a number of points on Parramatta Road to give motorists a chance to get on and off.
There is an exit planned near Wattle Street in Ashfield to allow motorists onto the nearby City West Link. Another is under consideration near Concord Oval, Burwood, and there is another planned at Taverners Hill.
It is understood there are other exits being looked at near Sydney University's Footbridge Theatre and near Redfern's Australian Technology Park, though these would be presented as options for the government if it had enough money to pay for them.
WestConnex's final estimated price tag is still likely to be about $10 billion, but... Some officials believe the estimated price has already climbed as high as $14 billion.
To offset the blowout in planned costs on the Parramatta Road section of WestConnex, cheaper options are being looked at on the southern side of the mammoth 33-kilometre project, closer to Sydney Airport and the existing M5 motorway.
