Motorists are being hit with Penalty Charge Notices of £130 - reduced to £65 when paid within two weeks - for entering cross-hatch yellow box junctions even if they weren't driving the car at the time.
Using a clause created in 2003 transport authorities are allowed to chase owners of vehicles, and not the drivers, to pay up for traffic violations committed on London roads, motoring title Honest John revealed following a series of investigations.
Daniel Powell, managing editor at Honest John, commented: "Whilst nobody would argue against why yellow boxes exist, using them as an excuse to extract cash from innocent motorists isn't what the Transport for London Act was created for."
According to decriminalisation measures in the Transport for London Act 2003, London Local Authorities have the power to chase down registered car owners for obstructing the city's traffic.
The act was set up to impose fines for unpaid parking violations, which are civil and not criminal offences, giving TfL the right to pursue vehicle owners for parking infringements even if they were not driving their car at the time.
